{
  "_PROVENANCE": "HONEST leakage-free re-evaluation of Species Health occupancy. The training script's per-site embedding positions 4-9 are log1p(per-species counts) which DEFINE the occupancy labels, so the previously reported AUROC=1.000 / occ_accuracy=0.999 / health_r2=0.9996 are circular (label read off the input). Here those per-species count features are REMOVED; only genuinely independent predictors (geographic, watershed, NON-target community context, and real matched sensor water-quality stats) are used. Probe = HistGradientBoosting classifier (+ logistic cross-check) on the leakage-free features, NOT the full network; this is an unbiased estimate of the achievable signal. Same real USGS BioData, same seed=42 spatial hash holdout split. Three leakage-free variants: A keeps a coarse HUC2 region one-hot + lat/lon; B drops the region one-hot (keeps lat/lon); C drops geography entirely (no lat/lon/region) to remove the species RANGE PRIOR and isolate non-geographic environmental signal. KEY FINDING: the rare species (eel, brook trout) are geographically range-restricted, so most of their AUROC comes from lat/lon (a real, count-independent range prior, NOT label leakage). The widespread mussel (16% prevalence) is the honest, informative case at ~0.85 AUROC, driven by NON-target community richness. Headline = Variant B; Variant C is the conservative no-geography floor.",
  "probe": "HistGradientBoostingClassifier (GBM) + LogisticRegression cross-check",
  "data_source": "USGS BioData (real biological surveys) + matched USGS sensor stations",
  "synthetic_data": false,
  "seed": 42,
  "split": "spatial hash holdout (sha256 of seed:site_id, mod 10)",
  "leaked_features_removed": "positions 4-9: per-species log1p(count) (define the labels)",
